WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.060 --> 00:00:05.290
Film Courage: What are two or three things
that a director cannot afford to get wrong

00:00:05.290 --> 00:00:08.400
because if they do, the movie is ruined?

00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:11.640
Mark W. Travis: Wow!

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.870
That’s a big question.

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:16.800
Two or three things they can’t get wrong…

00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:18.449
Film Courage: Right…cannot afford.

00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:20.109
Mark W. Travis: Okay.

00:00:20.109 --> 00:00:26.349
There is one thing Karen I think is really
important that I’ll include myself in this

00:00:26.349 --> 00:00:28.060
that we all fall into.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.279
A trap that we all fall into.

00:00:30.279 --> 00:00:35.040
And what I call it is the trap of assumption,
okay.

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:42.500
The trap of assumption is let’s say I have
a script and I really like it.

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:46.710
I’m moved by it, okay?

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:52.050
My assumption would be that other people will
it and be moved by it.

00:00:52.050 --> 00:00:55.860
Somebody else could read it, or see this film
or whatever and could be moved by it.

00:00:55.860 --> 00:00:58.080
I could be wrong.

00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:04.629
I’ll even take a scene between a husband
and wife and there is an argument going on

00:01:04.629 --> 00:01:09.640
and the husband is really angry at the wife
for some reason and to me he’s angry at

00:01:09.640 --> 00:01:11.429
her because of her attitude.

00:01:11.429 --> 00:01:12.429
Her impatience.

00:01:12.429 --> 00:01:14.939
I’ve done something very small.

00:01:14.939 --> 00:01:18.340
And my assumption is that anybody watching
it would get that.

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:22.090
It may not be true.

00:01:22.090 --> 00:01:28.509
The problem is this: when we’re looking
at a script, in a script, I’m just going

00:01:28.509 --> 00:01:32.630
to take one scene, I can read that scene Karen.

00:01:32.630 --> 00:01:34.360
And I can read it.

00:01:34.360 --> 00:01:39.240
Let’s say it’s just two pages, I can read
that scene and say “I got it.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:40.280
I can see what’s happening.

00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:42.610
I can feel the energy between the two characters.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:44.189
I can feel the conflict.

00:01:44.189 --> 00:01:45.439
I can feel the resolution.

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:46.600
I can feel all the obstacles.

00:01:46.600 --> 00:01:47.670
Everything that they’re going through.

00:01:47.670 --> 00:01:49.189
I get it.

00:01:49.189 --> 00:01:55.179
Now quite honestly those two pages, none of
what I felt is there.

00:01:55.179 --> 00:01:56.359
I can tell you that right now.

00:01:56.359 --> 00:01:57.619
None of it.

00:01:57.619 --> 00:01:59.770
None of the conflict and even the characters
is there.

00:01:59.770 --> 00:02:03.759
All it is, is words typed on a page.

00:02:03.759 --> 00:02:06.840
No different than a novel, it’s just words
typed on a page.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:12.980
What happens which we all have to recognize
is when you look at a script you project into

00:02:12.980 --> 00:02:18.230
that material what you believe is happening.

00:02:18.230 --> 00:02:23.750
Anybody who reads a script can suddenly hear
the characters, they can see the characters.

00:02:23.750 --> 00:02:24.930
Where is that coming from?

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:26.460
It’s not coming from the page.

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:27.460
It’s not there.

00:02:27.460 --> 00:02:29.140
There’s no pictures of the characters there.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:30.569
There’s no action there.

00:02:30.569 --> 00:02:31.569
Nothing.

00:02:31.569 --> 00:02:33.140
There is no emotion there.

00:02:33.140 --> 00:02:34.519
No performance there.

00:02:34.519 --> 00:02:35.519
Nothing.

00:02:35.519 --> 00:02:41.959
So what we do, what every human being does
is we project into those pages what we believe

00:02:41.959 --> 00:02:42.959
is happening.

00:02:42.959 --> 00:02:45.790
In other words, those pages are triggering
me.

00:02:45.790 --> 00:02:47.849
Those pages that I am reading trigger Mark.

00:02:47.849 --> 00:02:49.170
Mark’s history.

00:02:49.170 --> 00:02:51.870
Mark’s life experience, Mark’s expectation.

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:52.870
Everything.

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:53.870
It’s coming from me.

00:02:53.870 --> 00:02:55.099
So I project into it.

00:02:55.099 --> 00:02:56.989
Now Karen, you’ll do the same thing.

00:02:56.989 --> 00:03:01.110
But why should I assume that your projection
is the same as mine?

00:03:01.110 --> 00:03:03.319
That’s the danger of assumption.

00:03:03.319 --> 00:03:06.240
I’ll assume that you’ll get it the same
way I do.

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:07.240
Not true.

00:03:07.240 --> 00:03:08.240
Maybe close?

00:03:08.240 --> 00:03:10.280
I don’t know.

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.519
So the trap is the trap of assumption.

00:03:12.519 --> 00:03:17.030
I assume that you’re going to respond that
way.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:21.230
I could see a scene and go “That’s so
moving!” and you’re going “What?

00:03:21.230 --> 00:03:22.840
I don’t get it.”

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:24.670
My reaction was, it was moving.

00:03:24.670 --> 00:03:25.700
Your reaction was that it wasn’t moving.

00:03:25.700 --> 00:03:28.659
The assumption was you should get it.

00:03:28.659 --> 00:03:33.790
Thinking that just because it works for me,
it should work for everybody else.

00:03:33.790 --> 00:03:38.550
Yes, ironically, the flip side of that is
also true.

00:03:38.550 --> 00:03:41.879
I as a director have to make the movie for
me.

00:03:41.879 --> 00:03:45.069
I can’t make it for anybody else.

00:03:45.069 --> 00:03:49.019
So I have to allow a certain level of assumptions
are going on.

00:03:49.019 --> 00:03:52.219
I’m assuming because I’ve made a movie
that I really like that it is moving to me,

00:03:52.219 --> 00:03:55.909
I have to assume it is going to be moving
to enough people that lots of people are going

00:03:55.909 --> 00:03:57.829
to like it.

00:03:57.829 --> 00:04:01.599
So we’re trapped in the world of assumption.

00:04:01.599 --> 00:04:07.099
But trapped in that we have to do it but be
aware aware that it could be a problem.

00:04:07.099 --> 00:04:09.049
Film Courage: Okay.

00:04:09.049 --> 00:04:13.530
So how is someone aware of their own assumptions?

00:04:13.530 --> 00:04:20.799
Let’s suppose you think that these two pages
of very moving dialogue between two characters

00:04:20.799 --> 00:04:26.080
is just heart-wrenching because you see the
world that way or that scene, but how are

00:04:26.080 --> 00:04:31.060
you bouncing that off someone else…I don’t
know if I’m making myself clear?

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.120
Mark W. Travis: Yeah.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:34.960
I think so and I think I have an answer for
it.

00:04:34.960 --> 00:04:40.670
Let’s stay with this one scene because sometimes
just looking at one scene because a movie,

00:04:40.670 --> 00:04:42.009
you don’t just make a whole movie, as you
know.

00:04:42.009 --> 00:04:44.819
You make all these little scenes put them
together and it becomes a movie.

00:04:44.819 --> 00:04:49.630
So you have to be very focused, very myopic
about that scene.

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:53.770
So let’s say a scene between a husband and
wife.

00:04:53.770 --> 00:04:59.680
And getting back to you, how do I deal with
my assumptions.

00:04:59.680 --> 00:05:03.470
One of the biggest assumptions I as a director
can have, any director, every director will

00:05:03.470 --> 00:05:07.840
have…I read the scene, I know how it should
play.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:13.000
I, as the director know how this scene should
play.

00:05:13.000 --> 00:05:16.950
Should…now I am the director so I get to
decide.

00:05:16.950 --> 00:05:22.110
I get to decide how I want the actress to
play the mother and how I want the actor to

00:05:22.110 --> 00:05:23.669
play the father.

00:05:23.669 --> 00:05:34.110
Now this is what a lot of directors will do
and I can tell you right now because working

00:05:34.110 --> 00:05:38.289
with a lot of directors, I will say to a director
who says this is how it should go and I’ll

00:05:38.289 --> 00:05:39.780
say “How do you know?”

00:05:39.780 --> 00:05:46.190
“Well, that’s clearly the way it should
play” and I’ll talk to them about projection.

00:05:46.190 --> 00:05:47.319
So there is that assumption.

00:05:47.319 --> 00:05:51.010
Now we’re going to mix with that something
else which makes it even more difficult.

00:05:51.010 --> 00:05:53.740
You have the two actors.

00:05:53.740 --> 00:05:58.360
Now those two actors read the script and they’re
making assumptions.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:01.280
The actress will say “Oh, I know how to
play this.”

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:03.139
The actor will say “Oh, I know how to play
it.”

00:06:03.139 --> 00:06:08.139
Now we have three people who have different
points of view on how it should play and I

00:06:08.139 --> 00:06:12.069
can tell you right now the director, me and
those two actors, right?

00:06:12.069 --> 00:06:14.140
We’re all wrong.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:16.259
We’re all wrong.

00:06:16.259 --> 00:06:18.860
We’re right about our assumptions.

00:06:18.860 --> 00:06:20.729
We are right about our opinion.

00:06:20.729 --> 00:06:23.220
But we are wrong.

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:26.229
Our opinion actually does not matter.

00:06:26.229 --> 00:06:27.599
All three of us.

00:06:27.599 --> 00:06:34.190
The only opinion that matters is the opinion
of the characters.

00:06:34.190 --> 00:06:40.099
The characters, the husband and wife, they
know.

00:06:40.099 --> 00:06:42.819
And we’re trying to tell them how they should
behave.

00:06:42.819 --> 00:06:46.180
The actors are trying to tell them how to
behave by controlling them.

00:06:46.180 --> 00:06:52.759
I’m trying to tell the actors how to control
the characters, how to portray the characters.

00:06:52.759 --> 00:06:59.500
If I can get to the character, get to the
truth of the character my assumption may be

00:06:59.500 --> 00:07:02.190
blown right out of the water and the actress
may be, too.

00:07:02.190 --> 00:07:03.190
Or not.

00:07:03.190 --> 00:07:04.290
I don’t know.

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:09.970
But my job as the director is to serve the
characters.

00:07:09.970 --> 00:07:16.500
That’s getting back to what your question
was before, serve the story.

00:07:16.500 --> 00:07:19.789
Not my version of the story, their version
of the story.

00:07:19.789 --> 00:07:23.419
I want to get to the truth of who they are.

00:07:23.419 --> 00:07:27.509
I want to get to the truth of the characters
that the writer created that the writer is

00:07:27.509 --> 00:07:30.720
also making assumptions about.

00:07:30.720 --> 00:07:33.199
The writer may even tell me “Oh, it should
go this way.”

00:07:33.199 --> 00:07:35.440
And I say “Well I don’t know if you know
either?”

00:07:35.440 --> 00:07:39.759
Let’s get to the character and this gets
down to the interrogation process which is

00:07:39.759 --> 00:07:41.620
talking to the character.

00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:47.050
I want to know from the character what they
want, what they think, what they feel.

00:07:47.050 --> 00:07:51.349
The one thing is very important which may
have been in the DVD [Hollywood Film Directing:

00:07:51.349 --> 00:07:54.750
The Masterclass by Mark W. Travis] I gave
you, probably is.

00:07:54.750 --> 00:08:01.970
One thing that is very important, this is
a concept to me that is so clear and obvious

00:08:01.970 --> 00:08:07.680
but nobody talks about it because as soon
as I bring it up, people go “Oh, yeah!”

00:08:07.680 --> 00:08:09.250
The characters in your movie.

00:08:09.250 --> 00:08:10.879
The characters in this scene.

00:08:10.879 --> 00:08:17.610
These two people, husband and wife, whoever
they might be, are not in a movie.

00:08:17.610 --> 00:08:19.630
They’re not.

00:08:19.630 --> 00:08:23.030
They are two people who are in their life.

00:08:23.030 --> 00:08:27.670
They are in a moment in their life which happens
to be the scene trying to achieve whatever

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:29.439
they’re trying to achieve that is just two
people.

00:08:29.439 --> 00:08:31.610
They are not in a movie.

00:08:31.610 --> 00:08:36.550
They do not have a script.

00:08:36.550 --> 00:08:41.790
And we are trying to present them as authentically
as we can to a viewer.

00:08:41.790 --> 00:08:45.149
Well in order to do that we need to present
them as authentically as we can to a viewer.

00:08:45.149 --> 00:08:48.670
Well in order to do that, we need to honor
them, not my point of view necessarily.

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:52.200
Not the actors point of view necessarily but
them.

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:56.100
Let’s present them as authentically as we
can.

00:08:56.100 --> 00:09:03.589
I have learned so much about characters and
story by talking to the characters in a way

00:09:03.589 --> 00:09:09.000
(and I mean this seriously) dismissing a little
bit of what the writer says, what the actors

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.320
say, and even what I think.

00:09:11.320 --> 00:09:16.670
I go and I talk to the characters and I am
sometimes so educated and enlightened about

00:09:16.670 --> 00:09:18.670
who those people are.

00:09:18.670 --> 00:09:21.950
They are the ones I am here to serve.

00:09:21.950 --> 00:09:24.160
They are the ones that the writer is here
to serve.

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:28.560
And we’re all there in service of characters.

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:33.510
So let’s talk to them of the interrogation
process (which we can talk about later) as

00:09:33.510 --> 00:09:42.140
a way to get to them and get us, the writer,
the directors and the actors out of the way.

00:09:42.140 --> 00:09:49.370
Many times getting to the truth of the story,
the actors, the writers and the director are

00:09:49.370 --> 00:09:51.200
the biggest obstacle.

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:53.350
We are in the way of what we’re trying to
achieve.

00:09:53.350 --> 00:09:53.800
Question for the Viewers: What are your thoughts
on the “trap of assumption?"

